<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=flying-buttress">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flying buttress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1160s</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Arc-boutant, Arch buttress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A flying buttress is a ramping arch that carries the outward thrust of a stone vault or roof from the upper part of a wall across open space to a massive pier standing away from it. Ancient examples survive on the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna and the Rotunda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, and Romanesque churches such as Durham Cathedral hid similar arches under their gallery roofs. By the 1160s builders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> ran longer, visible arches over the aisles, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-germain-des-pres">Saint-Germain-des-Prés</a>, completed in 1163, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre Dame de Paris</a> in 1180. Walls could then be thinner, with larger windows of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a>, and the flying buttress became a defining feature of Gothic architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flying-buttress.jpg" alt="Flying buttress" width="330" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Andreas Praefcke · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lübeck_Marienkirche_Strebebögen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A flying buttress has two parts: a massive pier, a vertical block of masonry standing away from the building wall, and an arch, the flyer, that bridges the gap between pier and wall. It is called flying because it does not touch the wall at ground level, as an ordinary buttress does. It carries to the ground the lateral forces that arise from stone vaults and from wind on roofs. In Gothic churches two flyers were often set one above the other: the lower, below the springing of the vault, resisted the thrust of the vault, while the upper resisted the wind on the roof. The pier was usually capped with a pinnacle, often ornamented with crockets, whose weight added vertical load.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The system developed in late antiquity and flourished in the Gothic period, from the 12th to the 16th century. Early examples survive on the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna and the Rotunda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. Byzantine and Romanesque buildings supplied precursors. At Durham Cathedral arches carry the thrust of the aisle vault but are hidden under the gallery roof. By the 1160s architects in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> used longer, finer arches running over the roofs of the side aisles from the clerestory wall to a heavy pier, so that they were visible from outside. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-germain-des-pres">Saint-Germain-des-Prés</a>, completed in 1163, has them round its apse, and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre Dame de Paris</a>, built in 1180, were among the earliest on a Gothic cathedral.</p><p>To build one, carpenters made temporary wooden frames, called centring, on the ground, hoisted them into place and fixed them to the piers; these held the stones until the mortar cured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The advantage was that outer walls no longer had to be thick and heavy, so that wall surface could be reduced and larger windows, often of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a>, opened. Early flyers were heavier than needed, as at Chartres Cathedral around 1210, and later builders narrowed them, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>. Late Gothic flyers carried crockets and sculpted figures in niches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> architecture preferred thick walls and gave up the form, but in the early 20th century the Canadian engineer William P. Anderson revived it to build lighthouses. It has also been used to prop leaning walls, as at Chaddesley Corbett in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flying buttress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Several attributes set it apart from the earlier Romanesque style: its pioneering use of the rib vault and <b>flying buttress</b>, its enormous and colourful rose windows, and the naturalism and abundance of its sculptural decoration.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1160s</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Île-de-France (Saint-Germain-des-Prés, Paris)</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The flying buttress was first developed in late antiquity: early, undated examples stand on the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna and the Rotunda of Galerius in Thessaloniki.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/arch">Arch<span>before 3800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=flying-buttress">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
